Stop xmlsec1 declaring the same files twice.

    ERROR: One of the output paths '.../external/xmlsec1/xmlsec1/lib' and
    '.../external/xmlsec1/xmlsec1/lib/libxmlsec1.so.1' is a prefix of the
    other. These actions cannot be simultaneously present

The rule delivers the whole `lib` directory through out_data_dirs -- it has to,
because Bazel does not know about the .la files in there -- and *also* names
four libraries inside that directory in out_shared_libs. Bazel 5.4.1, which
upstream pins in .bazelversion, tolerated the overlap. Bazel 7 makes it an
artifact prefix conflict, and the release tarball ships no dotfiles, so the pin
never reaches this build: the image installs 7.4.0 and nothing reconciles them.

Two ways out, and this is the smaller one. The other four Bazel packages --
openssl, redis, patch, heirloom-mailx -- all build correctly under 7.4.0 in the
same run, so pinning the image back to 5.4.1 would move those four onto an
untested toolchain to fix the fifth.

Dropping the listing costs nothing. xmlsec1.make rsyncs the entire output tree,
so the libraries ship either way, and its next line runs patchelf over all four
of them -- a library that failed to build still stops the build, one step later
and with a clearer message than a missing Bazel output.

--- omd/packages/xmlsec1/BUILD.xmlsec1
+++ omd/packages/xmlsec1/BUILD.xmlsec1_v2
@@ -56,14 +56,11 @@
     # So instead of listing library files explicitly we just deliver the
     # whole `lib` folder, containing shared and dynamic libraries as well as
     # .la files
-    # Note that we list the dynamic libraries anyway, in order to fail if they
-    # for any reason can't be built
-    out_shared_libs=[
-        "libxmlsec1.so",
-        "libxmlsec1.so.1",
-        "libxmlsec1-openssl.so",
-        "libxmlsec1-openssl.so.1",
-    ],
+    # The libraries are deliberately not listed in `out_shared_libs` as well:
+    # declaring `lib` and files inside it is an artifact prefix conflict, which
+    # Bazel 7 rejects and the pinned 5.4.1 tolerated. Listing them was only a
+    # check that they got built, and that check survives elsewhere -- xmlsec1.make
+    # runs patchelf over all four, so one that is missing still stops the build.
     out_data_dirs=["lib"],
     targets=["install"],
 )
